  "version": 3,
  "name": "Joining-8",
  "description": "A figure-8 bend joining two ropes end to end, for when one rope isn't long enough.",
  "about": "In rescue it joins two ropes into one long enough for a long lower. The knot won't pass through most descenders or pulleys, so a joined rope needs a plan for getting the knot past them, called a knot pass.\n\nCheck that each rope follows the other's path side by side with no crossings. The two tails should leave from opposite ends of the knot, each lying alongside the other rope's standing part. Leave a tail of at least 100 mm on each rope.\n\nLoad it in line, with the two ropes pulling in opposite directions. Don't confuse it with the offset (flat) figure-8, where both ropes leave from the same side: that one can roll towards its tails and come undone. After a heavy load the joining-8 can jam hard.",
